Branch-local material for REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW ... WHERE ...
================================================================

This directory is NOT part of any test schedule and is not wired into either
build system.  Delete it before posting the patch to -hackers.  It holds the
things that do not belong in the patch itself:

  PLAN.md       Seven phases: what the tests need before the implementation can
                change safely, the change itself (string-built SQL through SPI
                -> Query trees), the performance work that only makes sense
                afterwards, and the path from there to a posted patch.  Read
                this second.  Phase 1 is complete; Phase 2 is next.

  ISSUES.md     Review items, split into those raised on the -hackers thread
                (A1-A9) and those found separately (B1-B20), each mapped to the
                test that covers it.  Start here.

  SAFETY.md     Which predicates are safe, established by differential testing
                rather than by argument.  Five independent failure modes, what
                the planner's own push-down analysis already decides, what is
                provable and what is not, and what a static check would cost.

  SPECIALIZE.md The Phase 4 design: one algorithm, several specialisations.
                Seven axes, which tier of the system can observe each, what
                each selects, and what must be declared rather than inferred.
                Written after the axis set changed three times in one sitting,
                each time invalidating a recommendation already made.

  USE-CASES.md  53 use cases across 17 domains, each placed against a measured
                cost model, with the driver pattern and refresh form that suits
                it.  Intended to feed the documentation and the design
                discussion.

All timings in these files come from a -O2 build with assertions off unless they
say otherwise.  An earlier revision quoted debug-build numbers under the caveat
"treat ratios as transferable"; that caveat did not hold, and several ratios
moved materially when re-measured.  Do not add a timing here without recording
which build produced it.


The instruments
---------------

Four of these are detectors and two are the scaffolding that makes them
trustworthy.  Every detector here has been shown to fail against a bug it is
meant to catch; that is the entry requirement, not a nicety.  Several of them
did not, at first, and looked exactly like working tests.

  safety/run.sh       The single-session differential oracle.  For each (view
                      shape, predicate, mutation): mutate the base, partial
                      refresh, snapshot, full refresh, symmetric difference.
                      22 exhaustive shapes over a 12-row base table -- 1636
                      mutations per refresh form, 3272 in total -- plus 42
                      single-shot triples carrying the planner-signal
                      measurement.  Answers "is this predicate safe".

  safety/rundiff.sh   Differential mode.  Two identical matviews, one refreshed
                      each way, diffed against each other.  Answers "do two
                      implementations agree", which is a different question:
                      both forms can be identically wrong on an unsafe shape and
                      this stays quiet, correctly.  The Phase 2 invocation is
                      `rundiff.sh 5610 postgres spi querytree` -- the text
                      implementation against the Query-tree one, selected by
                      matview_partial_refresh_querytree.

  fuzz.sh             The concurrent fuzzer, and the only thing here that can
                      see a bug needing two sessions.  Three modes: p2 and p3
                      for lock ordering over existing and inserted rows, serial
                      for whether overlapping refreshes actually serialize.
                      Probabilistic, and deleted at Phase 4.

  run.sh              The original A5 lock-ordering reproducer.  Superseded by
                      fuzz.sh's p2 mode, kept because it is the script the
                      -hackers thread refers to.

  mutations.py        Re-introduces known bugs one at a time, defined as edits
                      to `git show HEAD:` rather than to a copy kept somewhere.
                      It refuses to overwrite matview.c if the working copy is
                      not one of the variants it can itself produce -- it once
                      silently deleted two uncommitted assertions, and the
                      suites that ran green afterwards were green because there
                      was nothing left to check.

  rebuild.sh          Rebuilds and then proves the rebuild happened, by asking
                      the running server what it was compiled with.  autodepend
                      is empty in this tree, so a configure change relinks stale
                      objects and exits 0; that produced two silently wrong
                      builds before this existed.  A .c change needs --source;
                      a flag change needs --full, which runs make clean.

  calibrate.sh        Measures which known bugs the oracle detects, against a
  calibrate-fuzz.sh   recorded pristine vector rather than a hand-written list
                      of shapes to check.  The list that existed before this did
                      omit the one shape whose behaviour was unexplained.


What the calibration says
-------------------------

    A4  drop ON CONFLICT                 CAUGHT   oracle, via errs
    B4  anti-join NULL handling          CAUGHT   oracle, both NULLable shapes
    B6  wrong unique index               CAUGHT   oracle, via errs
    M1  drop ORDER BY, locking SELECT    CAUGHT   fuzz.sh p2
    M2  drop ORDER BY, new_data          CAUGHT   fuzz.sh p3
    M3  remove the locking SELECT        CAUGHT   fuzz.sh serial
    M6  lock after instead of before     CAUGHT   fuzz.sh serial
    M4  NOT MATERIALIZED                 MISSED   verified benign; nothing to catch

The oracle misses all four concurrency mutations and always will: it is
single-session, and a lock-ordering bug needs a second session to be a bug in.
That is the measurement that says fuzz.sh has to exist, not a defect in the
oracle.  Three of the four data bugs were invisible until the corpus grew a
shape for them -- a NULLable key, two unique indexes, and a text predicate
needing a collation -- and every one of those misses presented as a clean run.

    B19 unassigned collations           CAUGHT   oracle, shape 22 text_key

Which instrument fits which failure is the thing this directory keeps having to
relearn, and it comes down to one distinction:

    a rate      -> fuzz.sh.  Run it enough times and it happens.
    a window    -> an injection point.  It is microseconds wide; run it a
                   million times and it still will not happen by chance.

That was got wrong three times.  Most recently an insert-driven fuzzer mode was
built for the prune-vs-insert window, measured against a build with the bug
deliberately present, and reported a clean run; the deterministic gate for it is
injection_points/specs/matview-where-prune-gap.spec.

The oracle also found the use-after-free that is now B14, before it answered any
question about predicates.
